}

{
  "summary": "Higher‑multiplicity systems tend to host planets with shorter periods and lower masses, while single‑planet systems show longer periods and higher masses. Single‑planet planets average 2,642 days and 3.33 M⊕, compared to 1,192 days and 1.61 M⊕ for multi‑planet planets. Radial Velocity accounts for 53 % of detections and is more common in multi‑planet systems (≈41 % of its planets), whereas Transit detections are almost all single‑planet and suffer a ≈99 % missing‑mass rate. Pearson correlations between system multiplicity and orbital period (‑0.06) and mass (‑0.24) are weak, indicating little linear relationship. Mass data are missing for nearly half of all records, and the seven‑planet system has no mass measurements; extreme outliers (e.g., 730,000‑day periods) inflate the spread. Small sample sizes for high‑number groups and sparse mass values limit definitive conclusions.",
